<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Bakri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1094">1094</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Bakri was an Andalusian geographer and historian who wrote a description of North and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> completed around 1067. His account, based on reports from merchants and travellers, contains the most detailed early description of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a>, its capital, its king, its army and its trade in gold. It is a key source for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>'s history.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abū ʿUbayd ʿAbd Allāh al-Bakrī was an Arab Andalusian historian and geographer born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huelva">Huelva</a> about 1040. His father was sovereign of a short-lived principality that the family set up there when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> fell in 1031. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid">al-Mu'tadid</a>, the ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, deposed his father, the family moved to Córdoba, where al-Bakri studied with the geographer al-Udri and the historian Ibn Hayyan. He spent his whole life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a>, mostly in Seville and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almeria</a>, and was in Seville when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">El Cid</a> arrived to collect tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a>. He died in Córdoba in 1094 without ever having travelled to the places he described.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Al-Bakri wrote about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and the Arabian peninsula, but only two of his works survive. The Mu'jam mā ista'jam lists place names, mostly in the Arabian peninsula. His most important work is the Kitāb al-Masālik wa-al-Mamālik, the Book of Highways and of Kingdoms. It draws on earlier literature and on the reports of merchants and travellers, including Muhammad ibn Yūsuf al-Warrāq and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> ben Jacob. Some of al-Warraq's material dates from the 10th century, but al-Bakri also included recent events. For each area he describes the people, customs, geography, climate and main cities, and adds anecdotes. His works are noted for their relative objectivity. Parts of the main work are lost.</p><p>The book is one of the most important sources for the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> and gives crucial information on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravid dynasty</a> and the trans-Saharan trade. Of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> he wrote that the city consisted of two towns on a plain, and that the one inhabited by Muslims was large and had twelve mosques, with salaried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a> and muezzins, jurists and scholars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Al-Bakri recorded that Gao was one of the few early centres in the trans-Saharan trade along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> to have native Muslim inhabitants, and that other centres, among them Takrur, Songhay, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> territories, eventually followed. By the 11th century reports of such flourishing Islamic cities had reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">Al-Andalus</a>, which made his account possible. A crater on the Moon is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Bakri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Al-Bakri</b>'s celebrated description of the court, completed in 1068, recorded horses caparisoned in gold-embroidered cloth, pages bearing gold-mounted shields and swords, and even guard dogs wearing collars of gold and silver.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The family was cultured: <b>Abu Ubayd al-Bakri</b>, son of the last ruler, later wrote the Book of Roads and Kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-huelva">Taifa of Huelva</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huelva">Huelva</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.25,-6.95&amp;year=1084" aria-label="Huelva on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4376 823 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Al-Bakri"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4826 1093h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4826 1093h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.25,-6.95&amp;year=1084">What was here in 1084? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
